{
  "teamId": 146,
  "year": 2020,
  "narrative": "The 2020 Miami Marlins were a story nobody saw coming. Before the season even found its footing, the club was rocked by a COVID-19 outbreak that threatened to derail everything before it began. Yet this young, scrappy team — built on pitching, speed, and a handful of veterans — clawed its way to a winning record and the postseason in a shortened 60-game sprint, defying expectations at every turn.\n\nThe compressed August schedule was where the Marlins found their identity through chaos. Early home runs from unexpected heroes like Francisco Cervelli and rookie Monte Harrison offered glimpses of the offense Miami needed, while reliever Brandon Kintzler became the season's most tortured subplot — repeatedly surrendering crushing blows that threatened to sink the club. The bullpen's fragility was real, but the team somehow stayed afloat, going 13-14 in August — bruised but not broken.\n\nSeptember was where Miami proved it belonged. Starling Marte delivered a massive two-run double against Philadelphia, Jorge Alfaro came through in the clutch, and the Marlins leaned on timely pitching and defense — including a pivotal double play turned against the Yankees that helped lock up a playoff berth. The 16-14 September record was good enough to punch Miami's first postseason ticket since 2003, a genuine milestone for a franchise long mired in rebuilding.\n\nThe playoff run was brief but unforgettable. Corey Dickerson's three-run blast in the Wild Card round against the Cubs sent Marlins fans into a frenzy, and Miami dispatched Chicago to reach the NLDS against the Atlanta Braves. There, the young Fish met their match. Yimi García was touched up for a three-run homer by Travis d'Arnaud that turned the tide in Game 1, and Atlanta's powerful lineup proved too much. The NLDS exit stung, but the 2020 Marlins had already written something worth remembering.",
